\ Symbol literals are constructed directly at their stack slot in AOT mode:
\ the Value's tag, slice pointer, and length are written via ValueLayout
\ offsets, with the slice pointing at the binary's static const char[]. No
\ jitPushSymbol trampoline crossing and no per-literal allocation. A symbol
\ Value is structurally identical to a string Value -- same slice payload,
\ only the tag differs. Correct output proves the directly-constructed symbol
\ behaves identically to the interpreter across printing, inspect, equality,
\ inequality, and use as a hash key.
foo: .
foo: inspect print-line
foo: foo: = .
foo: bar: = .
H{ } foo: 1 @set bar: 2 @set foo: @get .
